<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Kodhai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andal was a Tamil poet-saint, probably of the 8th century, and the only woman among the twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a>, the saints devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> in the Bhakti movement. Tradition holds that Periyalvar found her as a child under a tulasi plant at Srivilliputhur and that she would marry no one but Vishnu. She is credited with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruppavai">Tiruppavai</a>, 30 verses in which she imagines herself one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s cowherd girls, and the Nachiyar Tirumoli, about 140 verses of longing for Vishnu. Both are recited during the Tamil month of Margazhi, in December and January.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andal.jpg" alt="Andal" width="320" height="608" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Saint_Andal_LACMA_M.86.94.2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to literary and religious tradition, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> devotee Periyalvar, who had no children, found a girl under a tulasi plant in the garden of the temple at Srivilliputhur and named her Kotai. She grew up as a devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. Tradition says that she wore a garland before it was offered to the temple's god, and that when Periyalvar reproached her, Vishnu appeared in his dream and asked that only the garland worn by Andal be dedicated to him. She was thus called the woman who wore and gave her garland. As she grew, she resolved to marry only Vishnu, thinking constantly of Ranganatha, his reclining form at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a>, and tradition holds that she was united with him there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Andal is credited with two Tamil works. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruppavai">Tiruppavai</a> is a collection of 30 verses in which she imagines herself a gopi, one of the cowherd girls devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. The verses describe a longing to serve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> through all eternity and the religious vows that she and the other girls will keep. The Nachiyar Tirumoli is a poem of 140 verses expressing her intense longing for Vishnu as her lover, using classical Tamil poetic conventions and stories from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>. Conservative Vaishnava institutions promote it less than the Tiruppavai because it belongs to an erotic genre of devotion, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadeva">Jayadeva</a>'s Gita Govinda.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Both works are recited during the winter month of Margazhi, and discourses on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruppavai">Tiruppavai</a> are given in Tamil, Telugu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and Hindi across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The Srivilliputhur Andal Temple consists of twin temples, one to Andal and one to Ranganatha, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> temples have a shrine to her. Festivals include the Adi Puram, which marks her adoption by Periyalvar, and the Panguni wedding festival. Garlands worn by Andal at Srivilliputhur are sent to the Venkateswara Temple at Tirumala. Historical records suggest that by the 12th century she was a major inspiration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> women in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>, and the Goda Mandali, a women's group founded in 1970, spreads her songs. Some modern interpreters read her choice of Vishnu over a human husband as feminist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…rites and devotional song, and some of the greatest bhakti poets, such as Mirabai and the Tamil saint <b>Andal</b>, were women; women also faced restrictions in the classical law books, and practices such as the burning of widows, sati, were opposed by reformers and banned under British rule…</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the Alvars, Nammalvar composed the Tiruvaymoli and <b>Andal</b> her songs of longing for Vishnu.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tiruppavai is a set of thirty Tamil Hindu hymns attributed to the female poet-saint <b>Andal</b>, one of the twelve poet-saints called the Alvars.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tiruppavai">Tiruppavai</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Srivilliputhur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.52,77.63&amp;year=749" aria-label="Srivilliputhur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6692 1808 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Andal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7142 2078h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7142 2078h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9.52,77.63&amp;year=749">What was here in 749 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
